1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Steel, for over a century, a symbol of America's might.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
Strong, durable, made to withstand great stresses.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:13,000
Steel created billionaires, a stock market explosion,

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:18,000
vertical architecture, weapons of war, a strong labor union.

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, American Steel, built to last on Modern Marvels,

6
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:25,000
then and now.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,860
By the 1950s, America was experiencing

8
00:00:32,861 --> 00:00:36,580
one of the greatest boons in the history of modern industry.

9
00:00:36,581 --> 00:00:41,780
Over 117 million tons of steel were produced in 1955,

10
00:00:41,781 --> 00:00:45,140
enough to make over 60 million automobiles.

11
00:00:45,141 --> 00:00:48,620
American steel was on top.

12
00:00:48,621 --> 00:00:51,380
Tens of thousands of workers made a handsome living

13
00:00:51,381 --> 00:00:55,460
in mills that were five, six, sometimes seven miles long.

14
00:00:55,461 --> 00:00:58,900
The incredible output of 100 million tons a year

15
00:00:58,901 --> 00:01:01,140
seemed almost easy.

16
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:04,380
While the industry itself has undergone major changes

17
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:07,000
over the years, the process of making steel

18
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:08,940
has remained remarkably consistent.

19
00:01:14,140 --> 00:01:20,020
Iron ore gives us iron, and iron is what's used to make steel.

20
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:22,020
The difference between iron and steel

21
00:01:22,120 --> 00:01:25,300
is the amount of carbon in each of them.

22
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:28,120
Steel is the product that results if carbon can be driven

23
00:01:28,121 --> 00:01:29,660
out of the iron.

24
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:31,620
The greatest challenge to making steel

25
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:34,420
was finding out a way to remove carbon from iron.

26
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:51,040
For over 5,000 years, civilizations

27
00:01:51,140 --> 00:01:56,180
have been struggling to unlock the secrets of iron and steel.

28
00:01:56,181 --> 00:02:00,520
The first use of iron really involved taking pieces

29
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:02,660
of meteorites.

30
00:02:02,760 --> 00:02:07,720
People were able to shape these fragments,

31
00:02:07,820 --> 00:02:10,720
these pieces of material, this metal from heaven,

32
00:02:10,820 --> 00:02:14,600
and it fascinated people.

33
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:17,700
King Tut's tomb, dating back to 3000 BC,

34
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:21,700
reveals the incredible value placed on iron.

35
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:23,500
While the casket was made of gold,

36
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:25,380
the iron implements of the young king

37
00:02:25,381 --> 00:02:27,520
were sewn into his garments.

38
00:02:27,620 --> 00:02:29,620
They included a mallet and a dagger.

39
00:02:33,880 --> 00:02:38,520
Iron was so valuable because of its mysterious origins.

40
00:02:38,620 --> 00:02:42,560
The discovery that iron existed just under the Earth's surface

41
00:02:42,660 --> 00:02:44,540
led to experiments which would soon

42
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:48,640
uncover some of the mysteries of iron.

43
00:02:48,740 --> 00:02:53,000
The entire business of forging, puddling iron

44
00:02:53,001 --> 00:02:55,400
was considered the work of an artisan,

45
00:02:55,500 --> 00:02:57,880
because so little was known scientifically

46
00:02:57,980 --> 00:03:00,540
that it was really handed down, often father to son.

47
00:03:03,940 --> 00:03:06,480
By the 13th century, there were two ways

48
00:03:06,580 --> 00:03:09,220
to transform iron into steel.

49
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:11,980
The first was to manually pound out the carbon

50
00:03:12,080 --> 00:03:13,820
and the other impurities.

51
00:03:13,920 --> 00:03:17,600
The second was to heat the iron to a temperature in excess

52
00:03:17,700 --> 00:03:20,820
of 2,500 degrees.

53
00:03:20,821 --> 00:03:24,020
This forced the carbon and other impurities out of the iron

54
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:27,320
and left steel behind.

55
00:03:27,420 --> 00:03:29,460
For centuries, craftsmen could rarely

56
00:03:29,560 --> 00:03:33,400
achieve the 2,500 degree heat required to drive the carbon

57
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:35,060
out of the iron.

58
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:38,800
Since even an open flame only reaches 500 degrees,

59
00:03:38,900 --> 00:03:42,080
oxygen was blown in from the side to fan the flames.

60
00:03:42,081 --> 00:03:48,220
During the Middle Ages, improved methods for increasing the heat

61
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:52,760
had been developed, but the manual process was still the only way

62
00:03:52,860 --> 00:03:55,200
that the carbon could be driven out.

63
00:03:59,260 --> 00:04:03,340
Hot liquid iron was strange and unpredictable.

64
00:04:03,440 --> 00:04:06,880
Those who knew its mysterious ways were treated like royalty.

65
00:04:06,980 --> 00:04:08,740
What's generally not understood is

66
00:04:08,741 --> 00:04:12,980
What's generally not understood is that steel is iron.

67
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:15,480
Through the ages, there were groups

68
00:04:15,580 --> 00:04:20,280
that figured out processes that were handed down

69
00:04:20,380 --> 00:04:22,580
from iron mongerer to iron mongerer

70
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:25,320
of making this refined type of iron that was steel,

71
00:04:25,420 --> 00:04:30,000
that was stronger, that wouldn't break during sword fights.

72
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:32,360
One group, the Damascus sword makers,

73
00:04:32,460 --> 00:04:36,660
toiled over hot flames for 14 to 16 hours a day.

74
00:04:36,661 --> 00:04:40,900
With brute force, the iron was pounded again and again,

75
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:42,500
the carbon driven out.

76
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:46,300
And a steel blade shaped like a sword was the result.

77
00:04:46,400 --> 00:04:50,500
One sword could take days to make.

78
00:04:50,600 --> 00:04:53,380
To test the blade, a slave was summoned,

79
00:04:53,480 --> 00:04:55,540
and his head was cut off.

80
00:04:55,640 --> 00:04:58,980
If the sword held its edge, it was ready for battle.

81
00:05:02,380 --> 00:05:04,580
No matter what the product, an ironsmith

82
00:05:04,581 --> 00:05:07,420
An iron smith had to be as tough as the metal with which he

83
00:05:07,520 --> 00:05:09,820
worked.

84
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:13,680
By the 1700s, England was the center of steel production.

85
00:05:13,780 --> 00:05:19,120
But soon, England's iron industry was in trouble.

86
00:05:19,220 --> 00:05:23,100
The problem was finding a fuel to heat the iron.

87
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:27,440
Wood from the forests was being depleted at an alarming rate.

88
00:05:27,540 --> 00:05:31,540
To make one ton of iron, it took 10 acres of forest.

89
00:05:31,640 --> 00:05:33,880
The tiny island nation that ruled the world

90
00:05:33,881 --> 00:05:36,720
found itself unable to meet its own energy needs.

91
00:05:40,220 --> 00:05:44,000
A new, undeveloped land across the ocean, America,

92
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:46,500
offered England her best hope.

93
00:05:46,600 --> 00:05:48,520
But America's vast natural riches

94
00:05:48,620 --> 00:05:49,900
would prove difficult to tap.

95
00:05:52,640 --> 00:05:55,400
Colonists built the first mill in 1622

96
00:05:55,500 --> 00:05:57,540
at Falling Creek, Virginia.

97
00:05:57,640 --> 00:06:00,100
They worked day and night pressing every man and woman

98
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:01,900
into service.

99
00:06:01,901 --> 00:06:03,800
The Native Americans living in the area

100
00:06:03,900 --> 00:06:08,600
were angered by the strange behavior of the intruders.

101
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:10,800
Determined to drive them out, the Native Americans

102
00:06:10,900 --> 00:06:14,880
attacked the mill just as the first fire was being stoked.

103
00:06:14,980 --> 00:06:17,980
All 348 members of the colony were killed,

104
00:06:18,080 --> 00:06:20,160
save for one young boy who escaped

105
00:06:20,260 --> 00:06:22,960
to tell a neighboring village the grim tale of the attack.

106
00:06:26,500 --> 00:06:29,920
22 years later, a mill was built in Saugus, Massachusetts,

107
00:06:29,921 --> 00:06:33,860
and the first American iron was made.

108
00:06:33,960 --> 00:06:36,820
The Industrial Revolution was just beginning,

109
00:06:36,920 --> 00:06:40,860
and colonial America was hungry for materials to build.

110
00:06:40,960 --> 00:06:44,860
But it became clear that the two types of iron, cast iron

111
00:06:44,960 --> 00:06:49,480
and wrought iron, had limitations.

112
00:06:49,580 --> 00:06:53,820
The cast iron is a very strong, however, relatively

113
00:06:53,920 --> 00:06:54,680
brittle material.

114
00:06:54,681 --> 00:06:57,580
material.

115
00:06:57,680 --> 00:07:01,880
Wrought iron is lower carbon, but it

116
00:07:01,980 --> 00:07:06,120
doesn't have the strength of the cast iron.

117
00:07:06,220 --> 00:07:08,900
For America to be transformed into a juggernaut

118
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,560
of industrial might, a way would have to be found

119
00:07:11,660 --> 00:07:15,560
to make steel affordable and easily available.

120
00:07:15,660 --> 00:07:19,480
And a brilliant entrepreneur was about to accidentally stumble

121
00:07:19,580 --> 00:07:23,000
onto a secret worth millions.

122
00:07:25,680 --> 00:07:28,680
American Steel now continues on Modern Marvels,

123
00:07:28,780 --> 00:07:29,680
Then and Now.

124
00:07:35,320 --> 00:07:39,520
By the late 1860s, the age of iron had arrived.

125
00:07:39,620 --> 00:07:42,540
The iron horse linked the coasts.

126
00:07:42,640 --> 00:07:46,340
Over 50,000 miles of track had been laid.

127
00:07:46,440 --> 00:07:50,640
Travel was easier, but it wasn't always safe.

128
00:07:50,740 --> 00:07:53,180
There would be what was called snakes,

129
00:07:53,181 --> 00:07:57,280
which would be an iron rail that would break out of its moorings

130
00:07:57,380 --> 00:08:01,420
on a tie and would swing up, come through the wooden frame

131
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:05,260
of a car and impale whatever was in its way.

132
00:08:05,360 --> 00:08:08,900
And this was one of the real awful and threats

133
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:11,060
of traveling in those days.

134
00:08:13,900 --> 00:08:17,280
Iron brought America into the Industrial Age,

135
00:08:17,380 --> 00:08:19,180
but a stronger metal would be needed.

136
00:08:23,180 --> 00:08:26,480
It took an Englishman, Henry Bessemer,

137
00:08:26,580 --> 00:08:30,420
to unlock the great mystery of steel.

138
00:08:33,360 --> 00:08:35,620
Bright and ambitious, Bessemer had shown early in his life

139
00:08:35,720 --> 00:08:38,700
a penchant for inspired invention.

140
00:08:38,800 --> 00:08:41,760
Bessemer, penniless and desperate to make money to marry,

141
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:44,600
invented a perforated stamp which put an end

142
00:08:44,700 --> 00:08:47,400
to the English practice of illegally reusing stamps

143
00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:49,000
over and over.

144
00:08:53,180 --> 00:08:57,320
His invention saved the British Post over 100,000 pounds a year

145
00:08:57,420 --> 00:08:58,980
and made him a wealthy man.

146
00:08:59,080 --> 00:09:02,420
But it was nothing compared to what was in store.

147
00:09:02,520 --> 00:09:05,660
Bessemer was about to solve one of the greatest mysteries

148
00:09:05,760 --> 00:09:08,320
of the industrial age.

149
00:09:08,420 --> 00:09:13,840
In England, they were producing this crucible steel.

150
00:09:13,940 --> 00:09:18,280
The question that was on everyone's mind

151
00:09:18,281 --> 00:09:24,380
was how could we produce a product comparable to, say,

152
00:09:24,480 --> 00:09:28,620
the crucible steel of Sheffield, England,

153
00:09:28,720 --> 00:09:33,920
on a large scale and cheaply.

154
00:09:34,020 --> 00:09:36,560
Bessemer had started on the road to his discovery

155
00:09:36,660 --> 00:09:39,700
by designing an improved artillery shell.

156
00:09:39,800 --> 00:09:42,400
The new bullet-shaped shell was an improvement

157
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:43,840
over the cannonball.

158
00:09:43,940 --> 00:09:47,000
But to be sent great distances, a stronger metal

159
00:09:47,001 --> 00:09:50,200
needed for the gun from which it was fired.

160
00:09:50,300 --> 00:09:52,540
Bessemer knew that steel would work,

161
00:09:52,640 --> 00:09:55,780
but in the 1850s, it was still virtually impossible

162
00:09:55,880 --> 00:09:59,540
to heat iron to the 2,500 degrees that were required

163
00:09:59,640 --> 00:10:01,120
to make steel.

164
00:10:01,220 --> 00:10:03,760
Bessemer made an unusual discovery.

165
00:10:03,860 --> 00:10:08,680
What he found is that by introducing a blow of cold air

166
00:10:08,780 --> 00:10:12,420
under certain conditions, that it would ignite the iron

167
00:10:12,421 --> 00:10:18,660
into twice its heat, up to 3,000 degrees Fahrenheit.

168
00:10:18,760 --> 00:10:24,700
And by doing that, he could drive off the carbon in the iron.

169
00:10:24,800 --> 00:10:27,100
And then by reintroducing it, could

170
00:10:27,200 --> 00:10:30,280
begin on a controlled basis to produce steel.

171
00:10:30,380 --> 00:10:33,740
He didn't understand all the metallurgical implications

172
00:10:33,840 --> 00:10:34,700
of what he was doing.

173
00:10:34,800 --> 00:10:35,740
In fact, no one did.

174
00:10:38,780 --> 00:10:40,980
The science was fairly simple.

175
00:10:40,981 --> 00:10:43,620
Bessemer's process was called a converter

176
00:10:43,720 --> 00:10:46,620
because the chemical process that took place literally

177
00:10:46,720 --> 00:10:49,780
converted iron to steel.

178
00:10:49,880 --> 00:10:53,000
Oxygen blown from underneath combined with the carbon

179
00:10:53,100 --> 00:10:56,760
and created a chemical reaction that created even greater heat.

180
00:10:56,860 --> 00:11:00,060
So effective was the conversion that all of the elements

181
00:11:00,160 --> 00:11:01,940
were driven from the iron.

182
00:11:02,040 --> 00:11:06,740
A small amount of carbon was reintroduced, creating steel.

183
00:11:10,980 --> 00:11:15,380
Many men would harness the new process in places like Chicago,

184
00:11:15,480 --> 00:11:19,380
Youngstown, and Sparrows Point.

185
00:11:19,480 --> 00:11:22,720
But one man would soon tower over them all.

186
00:11:33,240 --> 00:11:35,780
Andrew Carnegie was the poor immigrant son

187
00:11:35,880 --> 00:11:38,740
of a Scottish rag merchant, short in stature

188
00:11:38,741 --> 00:11:41,040
Short in stature but quick of wit,

189
00:11:41,140 --> 00:11:44,020
he'd come to America at age 11 and quickly embrace

190
00:11:44,120 --> 00:11:46,420
the values of his new home.

191
00:11:46,520 --> 00:11:49,320
He was a telegraph boy, and he began working

192
00:11:49,420 --> 00:11:52,420
for the Pennsylvania Railroad, but immediately caught the eye

193
00:11:52,520 --> 00:11:56,960
of the future president of the railroad, Tom Scott.

194
00:11:57,060 --> 00:12:01,600
It was through Scott and Scott's boss at the time, Edgar Thompson,

195
00:12:01,700 --> 00:12:05,340
that Carnegie got the money to build and to go

196
00:12:05,440 --> 00:12:08,240
into the steel industry.

197
00:12:08,241 --> 00:12:12,040
By his early 30s, Carnegie had amassed a sizable fortune

198
00:12:12,140 --> 00:12:14,480
as a manager for the railroad.

199
00:12:14,580 --> 00:12:18,480
His strategy was simple, invest in companies doing business

200
00:12:18,580 --> 00:12:22,280
with the railroad he represented.

201
00:12:22,380 --> 00:12:25,120
It was his investment in iron that brought him into what he

202
00:12:25,220 --> 00:12:28,300
believed would be the business of the future, steel.

203
00:12:31,700 --> 00:12:33,740
He began by buying steel mills.

204
00:12:33,840 --> 00:12:35,860
The capital was provided by investors

205
00:12:35,861 --> 00:12:37,900
he'd met as a railroad executive.

206
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:41,160
Soon he was gobbling up companies that sold coal and coke

207
00:12:41,260 --> 00:12:43,100
to heat the furnaces.

208
00:12:43,200 --> 00:12:45,700
And the mines that produced the iron ore.

209
00:12:45,800 --> 00:12:48,600
Carnegie's lessons with the railroad served him well

210
00:12:48,700 --> 00:12:50,900
in building his steel empire.

211
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:54,340
Carnegie was a finance man.

212
00:12:54,440 --> 00:12:56,340
He was not an engineer.

213
00:12:56,440 --> 00:12:58,280
He was not really an innovator.

214
00:12:58,380 --> 00:13:01,760
What he was is he put the various elements together.

215
00:13:01,860 --> 00:13:03,880
And he was a tremendous promoter.

216
00:13:05,860 --> 00:13:08,860
By controlling all aspects of production,

217
00:13:08,960 --> 00:13:12,200
Carnegie could keep prices low and profits high.

218
00:13:12,300 --> 00:13:17,300
But there was one cost that he couldn't control, labor.

219
00:13:17,400 --> 00:13:19,740
Initially a friend to labor, Carnegie

220
00:13:19,840 --> 00:13:22,780
found himself increasingly at odds with his workers.

221
00:13:25,440 --> 00:13:31,020
You had a process which takes the production of steel

222
00:13:31,021 --> 00:13:38,020
from the hands of the artisan and puts it into a mass

223
00:13:38,120 --> 00:13:45,960
production where you're able now to set up a process that no

224
00:13:46,060 --> 00:13:50,180
longer involves skilled and semi-skilled labor and are

225
00:13:50,280 --> 00:13:54,980
able to produce unlimited supplies of the most versatile

226
00:13:55,080 --> 00:13:57,280
engineering material.

227
00:13:57,281 --> 00:14:03,020
This had a tremendous impact on the world,

228
00:14:03,120 --> 00:14:07,020
on the technologies of America and Europe.

229
00:14:11,120 --> 00:14:13,700
Mass production was the order of the day

230
00:14:13,800 --> 00:14:16,040
in the industrial world.

231
00:14:16,140 --> 00:14:19,640
Mills on a massive scale had to be run 24 hours a day

232
00:14:19,740 --> 00:14:22,440
to maximize their potential.

233
00:14:22,540 --> 00:14:25,440
Mill owners like Carnegie wanted to cut wages.

234
00:14:25,441 --> 00:14:30,340
the workers were determined to hold onto their pay.

235
00:14:30,440 --> 00:14:33,180
The stage was set for one of the bloodiest disputes

236
00:14:33,280 --> 00:14:35,280
in the history of American business.

237
00:14:40,180 --> 00:14:43,600
Of every 10 pounds of metal produced in the world today,

238
00:14:43,700 --> 00:14:46,100
9 pounds are steel.

239
00:14:46,200 --> 00:14:49,400
Modern Marvels, then and now, will return in a moment.

240
00:14:49,401 --> 00:14:50,900
at the moment.

241
00:14:55,100 --> 00:14:58,400
American Steel now continues on Modern Marvels,

242
00:14:58,500 --> 00:14:59,340
then and now.

243
00:15:02,940 --> 00:15:07,160
In the late 19th century and well into the 20th century,

244
00:15:07,260 --> 00:15:10,660
American steel was the greatest industry on Earth.

245
00:15:10,760 --> 00:15:14,360
But the burden of greatness was born on the back of labor,

246
00:15:14,460 --> 00:15:17,560
and no man had benefited more from labor's efforts

247
00:15:17,561 --> 00:15:18,261
Carnegie.

248
00:15:21,660 --> 00:15:25,160
Carnegie had built the industry by cutthroat competition

249
00:15:25,260 --> 00:15:29,240
and by controlling virtually all aspects of production.

250
00:15:29,340 --> 00:15:33,000
But his influence didn't stop inside the mill.

251
00:15:33,100 --> 00:15:35,540
He fostered a system that would last well

252
00:15:35,640 --> 00:15:37,980
into the 20th century.

253
00:15:38,080 --> 00:15:40,320
The relationship between the company

254
00:15:40,420 --> 00:15:44,660
and the citizens of the town was very much like a feudal

255
00:15:44,661 --> 00:15:47,600
between lords and serfs.

256
00:15:47,700 --> 00:15:51,600
People were dependent often on the company store.

257
00:15:51,700 --> 00:15:55,240
Their homes were often bought through the company

258
00:15:55,340 --> 00:15:59,700
so that politically it was hard for people to organize,

259
00:15:59,800 --> 00:16:02,740
for example, out of fear of losing their livelihoods

260
00:16:02,840 --> 00:16:05,420
and losing their homes.

261
00:16:05,520 --> 00:16:08,580
Life in a mill town was hard.

262
00:16:08,680 --> 00:16:11,780
The steel towns were very smoky.

263
00:16:11,781 --> 00:16:16,780
workers often suffered from diseases like porousy.

264
00:16:16,880 --> 00:16:20,520
You can imagine how hot a steel mill would be,

265
00:16:20,620 --> 00:16:23,520
and then in the middle of winter in Pittsburgh,

266
00:16:23,620 --> 00:16:28,620
going from this extraordinary heat into winter cold.

267
00:16:28,720 --> 00:16:32,340
While they had state-of-the-art equipment,

268
00:16:32,440 --> 00:16:34,440
this was not benign equipment.

269
00:16:34,540 --> 00:16:37,880
There was a high number of dangers in these mills.

270
00:16:37,980 --> 00:16:40,480
In fact, there were arguably much greater dangers

271
00:16:40,481 --> 00:16:42,280
because the mills were huge.

272
00:16:42,380 --> 00:16:45,920
There were cranes overhead, trains shuttling back and forth.

273
00:16:46,020 --> 00:16:47,720
There was deafening noise.

274
00:16:47,820 --> 00:16:51,600
There was intense heat and sparks.

275
00:16:51,700 --> 00:16:54,800
The steel industry got more and more dangerous

276
00:16:54,900 --> 00:17:00,060
as it became more and more advanced in terms of its product.

277
00:17:00,160 --> 00:17:04,000
Metal boiling at 2,500 degrees could drive temperatures

278
00:17:04,100 --> 00:17:07,000
inside the mill well over 100 degrees

279
00:17:07,001 --> 00:17:10,100
and make life unbearable for the worker.

280
00:17:10,200 --> 00:17:13,840
Sweating provided the only natural defense.

281
00:17:13,940 --> 00:17:17,740
Popular lore has it of steel workers stripped to the waist

282
00:17:17,840 --> 00:17:21,840
and sweating over these pots of metal.

283
00:17:21,940 --> 00:17:26,280
The reality was is they were swathed in sweaters,

284
00:17:26,380 --> 00:17:28,620
in cloth, in gloves.

285
00:17:28,720 --> 00:17:32,500
They covered everything because the sparks that would come out

286
00:17:32,501 --> 00:17:38,100
as they quickly would dart in to throw in some coke, dolomite,

287
00:17:38,200 --> 00:17:40,580
or other ingredient that was needed,

288
00:17:40,680 --> 00:17:42,580
the sparks would come out and burn you.

289
00:17:42,680 --> 00:17:46,940
Many of the men believed in drinking steaming hot coffee

290
00:17:47,040 --> 00:17:48,980
all day long, even in the dead of summer,

291
00:17:49,080 --> 00:17:51,920
because it would keep you at this sweating.

292
00:17:54,760 --> 00:17:58,200
Making steel was unlike any other job.

293
00:17:58,201 --> 00:18:01,200
job.

294
00:18:01,300 --> 00:18:04,440
A greenhorn wouldn't be told on his first day at work

295
00:18:04,540 --> 00:18:07,380
that he shouldn't keep his spare change in his pocket.

296
00:18:07,480 --> 00:18:09,640
He would get little burn marks.

297
00:18:09,740 --> 00:18:13,080
And there are many great jokes about the different types

298
00:18:13,180 --> 00:18:16,180
of food that the wives would pack for the workmen,

299
00:18:16,280 --> 00:18:20,020
about how a sandwich that would be packed would turn green

300
00:18:20,120 --> 00:18:21,420
within an hour in the mill.

301
00:18:21,421 --> 00:18:29,920
BILL KURTIS- In 1890, Homestead, Pennsylvania,

302
00:18:30,020 --> 00:18:32,200
just six miles outside Pittsburgh,

303
00:18:32,300 --> 00:18:34,000
was like many other steel towns.

304
00:18:41,100 --> 00:18:44,580
Mills like Homestead were running virtually 24 hours a day

305
00:18:44,680 --> 00:18:48,040
in order to meet the railroad's voracious demand for steel

306
00:18:48,140 --> 00:18:51,420
rails to replace the iron rails.

307
00:18:51,421 --> 00:18:56,420
By 1890, Carnegie controlled virtually every aspect

308
00:18:56,520 --> 00:19:00,160
of the steelmaking process except one, labor.

309
00:19:00,260 --> 00:19:03,440
Steel rails were not only better than iron rails.

310
00:19:03,540 --> 00:19:06,540
They were around half the price.

311
00:19:06,640 --> 00:19:11,680
That, in turn, caused pressure on labor wages.

312
00:19:11,780 --> 00:19:15,520
The key thing, although it could not be stated publicly

313
00:19:15,620 --> 00:19:18,640
at the time, was to break the amalgamated iron and steel

314
00:19:18,641 --> 00:19:21,880
iron and steel worker, which was then a very strong union.

315
00:19:21,980 --> 00:19:24,580
And at Homestead, they were very strong.

316
00:19:24,680 --> 00:19:26,380
So strong, in fact, that Homestead

317
00:19:26,480 --> 00:19:29,980
was to become the battlefield upon which the war over wages

318
00:19:30,080 --> 00:19:31,580
would be fought.

319
00:19:31,680 --> 00:19:33,480
Events were about to unfold which

320
00:19:33,580 --> 00:19:35,820
would determine the future of the steel worker

321
00:19:35,920 --> 00:19:37,400
for the next half century.

322
00:19:40,260 --> 00:19:43,040
If workers at Homestead could be brought to heel,

323
00:19:43,140 --> 00:19:45,700
other mills would follow.

324
00:19:45,701 --> 00:19:48,600
Both sides had reason to mistrust the other.

325
00:19:48,700 --> 00:19:51,100
The workers felt constant pressure from management

326
00:19:51,200 --> 00:19:53,200
to accept lower pay.

327
00:19:53,300 --> 00:19:56,940
Traditionally, workers were paid by the ton.

328
00:19:57,040 --> 00:20:01,780
A steel worker could make anywhere from $35 to $85 a month,

329
00:20:01,880 --> 00:20:04,660
depending on the skill level.

330
00:20:04,760 --> 00:20:06,860
This at a time when a modest home

331
00:20:06,960 --> 00:20:08,960
rented for about $5 a month.

332
00:20:11,760 --> 00:20:14,960
As the equipment improved and the tonnage increased,

333
00:20:14,961 --> 00:20:18,060
workers believed that wages should follow.

334
00:20:18,160 --> 00:20:22,960
Instead, management tried to cut wages to boost profit.

335
00:20:23,060 --> 00:20:25,700
Carnegie felt pay cuts were justified.

336
00:20:25,800 --> 00:20:30,000
After all, he had spent vast sums to modernize his plants.

337
00:20:34,380 --> 00:20:36,780
It was the new machines, not the men,

338
00:20:36,880 --> 00:20:41,020
that increased productivity and thus profit.

339
00:20:41,120 --> 00:20:42,960
To work with steel, the worker no longer

340
00:20:42,961 --> 00:20:45,260
needed to be a highly skilled craftsman.

341
00:20:45,360 --> 00:20:49,300
He was just another part of the machine.

342
00:20:49,400 --> 00:20:51,460
Both sides believed they were right,

343
00:20:51,560 --> 00:20:55,600
and it was at Homestead that the battle lines would be drawn.

344
00:21:06,120 --> 00:21:10,620
Carnegie put the problem into the hands of his trusted associate,

345
00:21:10,621 --> 00:21:13,020
Henry Clay Frick.

346
00:21:13,120 --> 00:21:16,560
Frick was a fascinating, albeit reticent, man.

347
00:21:19,660 --> 00:21:22,700
His grandfather was a whiskey maker.

348
00:21:22,800 --> 00:21:28,300
And Frick was a steer, penny-pinching Puritan,

349
00:21:28,400 --> 00:21:30,940
who was sort of the alter ego of Carnegie.

350
00:21:33,540 --> 00:21:35,940
Carnegie was much more personable.

351
00:21:36,040 --> 00:21:37,820
He was much more outgoing.

352
00:21:37,920 --> 00:21:39,680
He pictured himself as a person who

353
00:21:39,681 --> 00:21:43,380
who had good relations with his workers.

354
00:21:43,480 --> 00:21:47,620
Frick was much sterner.

355
00:21:47,720 --> 00:21:50,960
Carnegie saw in Frick a competent manager

356
00:21:51,060 --> 00:21:56,100
and named him president of Homestead on July 1, 1891,

357
00:21:56,200 --> 00:21:59,940
the same day that the union at Homestead had set as the deadline

358
00:22:00,040 --> 00:22:02,300
for wage negotiations.

359
00:22:02,400 --> 00:22:05,700
Carnegie skedaddled back to Scotland.

360
00:22:05,701 --> 00:22:09,300
Clearly, Carnegie wanted to break that union,

361
00:22:09,400 --> 00:22:11,040
but he did not want to do it personally.

362
00:22:11,140 --> 00:22:15,580
So he put that into the hands of Henry Clay Frick.

363
00:22:15,680 --> 00:22:18,980
And during the homestead strike, Frick called all the orders

364
00:22:19,080 --> 00:22:20,020
and took all the heat.

365
00:22:22,680 --> 00:22:26,480
When the workers walked out, all eyes were on Frick.

366
00:22:26,580 --> 00:22:28,720
Everyone wanted to see what Frick would do.

367
00:22:31,760 --> 00:22:34,460
His first action was to lock the man out of the mill.

368
00:22:34,461 --> 00:22:37,160
it was a clear message.

369
00:22:37,260 --> 00:22:39,400
Scabs would be brought in to break the strike.

370
00:22:42,660 --> 00:22:45,400
For three generations, the sweat from the steelworkers'

371
00:22:45,500 --> 00:22:47,400
brows had built Homestead.

372
00:22:47,500 --> 00:22:50,780
They would protect it at all cost.

373
00:22:50,880 --> 00:22:52,380
Frick had a secret plan.

374
00:22:52,480 --> 00:22:55,440
He would bring in 300 armed guards from the Pinkerton

375
00:22:55,540 --> 00:22:58,820
Detective Agency to guard the works.

376
00:22:58,920 --> 00:23:04,020
I think it's clear that Carnegie, Frick, and the workers

377
00:23:04,021 --> 00:23:07,060
The workers themselves understood that what the homestead strike

378
00:23:07,160 --> 00:23:10,120
was about was not so much about the contract.

379
00:23:10,220 --> 00:23:12,960
It was about the existence of the union itself.

380
00:23:13,060 --> 00:23:17,500
It was about the destruction of the craft.

381
00:23:17,600 --> 00:23:21,440
The strike was about the company getting control over the process

382
00:23:21,540 --> 00:23:23,800
rather than the workers having control over it.

383
00:23:26,640 --> 00:23:29,320
The workers were determined to keep outsiders

384
00:23:29,420 --> 00:23:30,420
from entering the mill.

385
00:23:30,421 --> 00:23:33,920
Arriving at homestead, the Pinkertons

386
00:23:34,020 --> 00:23:37,660
found angry workers lying in wait.

387
00:23:37,760 --> 00:23:40,200
The Pinkertons were ordered not to shoot

388
00:23:40,300 --> 00:23:42,160
unless the strikers shot first.

389
00:23:42,260 --> 00:23:47,140
In all, over 10,000 angry laborers

390
00:23:47,240 --> 00:23:50,040
had gathered to protect the mill.

391
00:23:50,140 --> 00:23:55,240
Slowly, the armed guards filed off the boat, shotguns in hand.

392
00:23:55,340 --> 00:23:58,180
For a moment, all was still.

393
00:24:00,420 --> 00:24:01,820
And then a shot.

394
00:24:01,920 --> 00:24:05,160
To this day, no one is sure who fired first.

395
00:24:05,260 --> 00:24:08,560
But the ensuing battle cost the lives of three Pinkerton

396
00:24:08,660 --> 00:24:11,300
detectives and five steel workers.

397
00:24:11,400 --> 00:24:14,860
Over 90 were injured.

398
00:24:14,960 --> 00:24:17,880
The first bloody outburst would also be the last.

399
00:24:17,980 --> 00:24:19,300
The Pinkertons surrendered.

400
00:24:19,400 --> 00:24:23,940
And for a short time, it looked as though the workers had won.

401
00:24:24,040 --> 00:24:27,140
For Frick, it had been a public relations fiasco,

402
00:24:27,141 --> 00:24:30,080
and he had lost control of the mill.

403
00:24:30,180 --> 00:24:32,880
And Carnegie's name would forever be tarnished

404
00:24:32,980 --> 00:24:34,080
by the bloody dispute.

405
00:24:36,980 --> 00:24:39,380
On July 6, the governor of Pennsylvania

406
00:24:39,480 --> 00:24:41,920
ordered 8,000 troops from the state militia

407
00:24:42,020 --> 00:24:45,260
to return control of the mill at Homestead to Frick.

408
00:24:45,360 --> 00:24:46,660
The rioting was over.

409
00:24:46,760 --> 00:24:51,260
But in one last bizarre twist, more blood would be shed.

410
00:24:51,360 --> 00:24:54,500
And it would be Frick's.

411
00:24:54,600 --> 00:24:56,600
He was working in his office on the afternoon

412
00:24:56,601 --> 00:25:00,100
of July 10th, when a lone gunman burst into the room.

413
00:25:00,200 --> 00:25:03,100
He pulled a gun and shot Frick twice in the neck.

414
00:25:03,200 --> 00:25:06,480
Miraculously, Frick survived.

415
00:25:06,580 --> 00:25:09,980
For the surgery that followed, he even refused anesthetic

416
00:25:10,080 --> 00:25:12,220
and then returned to work to sign some papers

417
00:25:12,320 --> 00:25:14,720
later that same day.

418
00:25:14,820 --> 00:25:16,920
The attacker had been a Russian anarchist,

419
00:25:17,020 --> 00:25:19,460
not connected with the strike.

420
00:25:19,560 --> 00:25:23,660
Frick gained much needed sympathy from the public.

421
00:25:23,661 --> 00:25:27,460
He was simply a man doing his job.

422
00:25:27,560 --> 00:25:31,440
Carnegie was now seen by the public as the real culprit.

423
00:25:31,540 --> 00:25:34,540
Frick agreed.

424
00:25:34,640 --> 00:25:38,500
Frick was silently disapproving of Carnegie's position

425
00:25:38,600 --> 00:25:40,340
on labor.

426
00:25:40,440 --> 00:25:45,020
Carnegie, in public, presented himself as a friend of labor,

427
00:25:45,120 --> 00:25:48,920
but privately he benefited from the stance

428
00:25:49,020 --> 00:25:51,520
that Frick took against the workers at Homestead.

429
00:25:53,660 --> 00:25:58,660
When the workers were ready to return to the table, Frick refused to negotiate.

430
00:25:58,661 --> 00:26:01,660
The men would come back to work on his terms.

431
00:26:01,661 --> 00:26:06,660
Unable to maintain the strike, the workers had little choice but to accept.

432
00:26:10,660 --> 00:26:14,660
Carnegie's grip on the steel industry was now absolute.

433
00:26:14,661 --> 00:26:19,660
He was about to become the richest man in the world, but Carnegie had even greater goals.

434
00:26:19,661 --> 00:26:22,900
He built the industry that would build America,

435
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:25,500
but he wasn't finished building his dream.

436
00:26:29,600 --> 00:26:32,600
American Steel now continues on Modern Marvels,

437
00:26:32,700 --> 00:26:33,500
then and now.

438
00:26:37,120 --> 00:26:39,520
At the turn of the century, America

439
00:26:39,620 --> 00:26:42,180
was humming with the sounds of progress,

440
00:26:42,280 --> 00:26:45,620
and the nation's growth was fueled by the strongest material

441
00:26:45,720 --> 00:26:47,560
on Earth, steel.

442
00:26:47,561 --> 00:26:52,800
And at the center of it all sat Andrew Carnegie.

443
00:26:52,900 --> 00:26:55,060
Not only had Carnegie built the industry,

444
00:26:55,160 --> 00:26:58,680
he also found new uses for steel products.

445
00:26:58,780 --> 00:27:00,880
The home insurance building in Manhattan

446
00:27:00,980 --> 00:27:03,580
was a good example.

447
00:27:03,680 --> 00:27:06,380
At first, the architects were reluctant to build

448
00:27:06,480 --> 00:27:08,980
higher than the usual six stories.

449
00:27:09,080 --> 00:27:12,980
But Carnegie suggested using a new steel beam reinforced

450
00:27:13,080 --> 00:27:15,320
at the bottom.

451
00:27:15,321 --> 00:27:17,120
This way, the weight of the building

452
00:27:17,220 --> 00:27:20,560
could be distributed evenly through the walls.

453
00:27:20,660 --> 00:27:24,260
It was an ingenious solution and an instant success.

454
00:27:28,860 --> 00:27:31,400
Once the Otis Elevator Company figured out a way

455
00:27:31,500 --> 00:27:35,480
to speed people to high floors, the race of the skyscrapers

456
00:27:35,580 --> 00:27:36,280
was on.

457
00:27:36,281 --> 00:27:37,080
was on.

458
00:27:42,760 --> 00:27:46,060
Steel would support the greatest buildings on Earth

459
00:27:46,160 --> 00:27:49,120
and make possible the American skyline of the 20th century.

460
00:27:52,260 --> 00:27:57,160
In 1900, at age 62, Carnegie did what he had vowed he would

461
00:27:57,260 --> 00:27:58,260
never do.

462
00:27:58,360 --> 00:28:03,480
He sold his company to his arch rival, J.P. Morgan.

463
00:28:03,481 --> 00:28:06,420
The sale made him one of the richest men in the world,

464
00:28:06,520 --> 00:28:11,380
with a fortune in excess of $350 million, over $10 billion

465
00:28:11,480 --> 00:28:13,120
today.

466
00:28:13,220 --> 00:28:17,620
He sold the company to fulfill his last great dream, the dream

467
00:28:17,720 --> 00:28:20,540
of giving all his money away.

468
00:28:20,640 --> 00:28:23,440
But Carnegie was so wealthy that his money earned interest

469
00:28:23,540 --> 00:28:26,740
at a rate faster than it could be given away.

470
00:28:26,840 --> 00:28:30,640
Nevertheless, he would spend the rest of his days trying.

471
00:28:33,480 --> 00:28:36,520
J.P. Morgan added his own holdings to the Carnegie

472
00:28:36,620 --> 00:28:40,480
acquisition and called the new company US Steel.

473
00:28:40,580 --> 00:28:44,320
He went public with the stock, and US Steel became so powerful

474
00:28:44,420 --> 00:28:49,460
that within nine months, it had boosted the Dow Jones by 500%.

475
00:28:49,461 --> 00:29:03,461
The sale of Carnegie Steelworks made way for one of the most

476
00:29:05,600 --> 00:29:09,120
fascinating personalities in the steel business.

477
00:29:09,220 --> 00:29:12,820
Charlie Schwab was a born entrepreneur

478
00:29:12,920 --> 00:29:15,920
with a showman's flair.

479
00:29:16,020 --> 00:29:18,460
Charlie Schwab was born in Loretto, Pennsylvania,

480
00:29:18,461 --> 00:29:22,460
an extremely remote outpost at the top of the Allegheny

481
00:29:22,560 --> 00:29:26,260
Mountains that was founded by a Russian prince turned

482
00:29:26,360 --> 00:29:30,600
Catholic priest who, after the 1800s,

483
00:29:30,700 --> 00:29:33,580
moved a small group of German immigrants

484
00:29:33,680 --> 00:29:37,440
to a small village whose aim was to withdraw itself

485
00:29:37,540 --> 00:29:39,740
from the Industrial Revolution.

486
00:29:39,840 --> 00:29:43,680
Well, good old Charlie didn't really cotton much to this.

487
00:29:43,780 --> 00:29:45,660
Schwab, no relation to the founder

488
00:29:45,760 --> 00:29:47,860
of today's financial institution,

489
00:29:47,861 --> 00:29:51,820
left his small town and landed at the Edgar Thompson Steelworks.

490
00:29:52,320 --> 00:29:55,180
He showed promise and quickly rose through the ranks.

491
00:29:55,820 --> 00:29:58,560
Young Schwab made an immediate impression on Carnegie,

492
00:29:58,780 --> 00:30:02,780
who perhaps saw a little of himself in the polite but aggressive lad.

493
00:30:03,660 --> 00:30:08,580
By his early 30s, Schwab had become one of Carnegie's most trusted and valued managers.

494
00:30:11,460 --> 00:30:14,440
Of Schwab, Carnegie would later remember,

495
00:30:14,441 --> 00:30:16,240
Charlie is a genius.

496
00:30:16,241 --> 00:30:18,380
I have never met his equal.

497
00:30:18,480 --> 00:30:21,580
And when he had me as his partner, we were a great team.

498
00:30:24,880 --> 00:30:27,280
In Carnegie, Schwab had a mentor.

499
00:30:27,380 --> 00:30:30,080
But with Morgan, the situation was different.

500
00:30:30,180 --> 00:30:35,300
Rumors about Schwab's gambling made Morgan nervous.

501
00:30:35,400 --> 00:30:39,400
A large company like US Steel needed a strong, steady hand

502
00:30:39,500 --> 00:30:40,800
at the helm.

503
00:30:40,900 --> 00:30:44,100
As reward for bringing Carnegie and Morgan together,

504
00:30:44,101 --> 00:30:46,400
Schwab had been appointed president,

505
00:30:46,500 --> 00:30:50,440
but Schwab had competition.

506
00:30:50,540 --> 00:30:52,580
Charlie Schwab was the president,

507
00:30:52,680 --> 00:30:55,840
but somewhat to check him under the Morgan system

508
00:30:55,940 --> 00:30:59,320
was a lawyer named Albert Gehry.

509
00:30:59,420 --> 00:31:03,080
And he too was taken aback by Schwab.

510
00:31:03,180 --> 00:31:07,960
Gehry basically told Schwab that he had to keep a very low

511
00:31:08,060 --> 00:31:09,420
profile.

512
00:31:09,421 --> 00:31:14,820
Schwab insulted, refused, and left the company.

513
00:31:14,920 --> 00:31:18,960
But Schwab was determined to have the last word.

514
00:31:19,060 --> 00:31:22,000
So furious was Schwab at being rejected

515
00:31:22,100 --> 00:31:24,640
and such personal animosity to Gary

516
00:31:24,740 --> 00:31:28,100
that he began charting his comeback.

517
00:31:28,200 --> 00:31:32,440
And he saw as his instrument Bethlehem Steel.

518
00:31:32,540 --> 00:31:36,440
Bethlehem Steel was a small supplier of armor plate.

519
00:31:36,441 --> 00:31:40,680
But Schwab saw a big potential.

520
00:31:40,780 --> 00:31:44,480
And he vowed to reporters who listened to him around 1904

521
00:31:44,580 --> 00:31:49,580
that he would make this company the rival of US steel.

522
00:31:49,680 --> 00:31:53,420
Between 1907 and 1914, Schwab built Bethlehem

523
00:31:53,520 --> 00:31:54,960
into a first-rate supplier.

524
00:31:58,460 --> 00:32:01,300
A new process helped increase productivity.

525
00:32:01,400 --> 00:32:03,840
It was called the open hearth system.

526
00:32:03,841 --> 00:32:06,840
The open hearth utilized scrap steel.

527
00:32:06,940 --> 00:32:11,240
Scrap was dumped into the furnace and iron ore poured on top.

528
00:32:11,340 --> 00:32:17,180
Oxygen and hot air were then forced into the hearth from the side.

529
00:32:17,280 --> 00:32:20,820
A brick-lined chamber on the other side captured the heat,

530
00:32:20,920 --> 00:32:24,400
which was then reused by reversing the flow of oxygen

531
00:32:24,500 --> 00:32:27,000
and hot air over the bricks.

532
00:32:27,100 --> 00:32:29,100
Scientific knowledge had improved,

533
00:32:29,200 --> 00:32:32,060
and precise amounts of trace elements could be added

534
00:32:32,061 --> 00:32:35,600
could be added to make the perfect mixture of steel.

535
00:32:35,601 --> 00:32:37,980
Production increased tenfold,

536
00:32:37,981 --> 00:32:40,060
but Schwab's success was nothing compared

537
00:32:40,061 --> 00:32:42,540
to what was about to happen with a little help

538
00:32:42,541 --> 00:32:43,760
from the Great War.

539
00:32:55,980 --> 00:33:00,380
By 1914, the shadow of war darkened Europe.

540
00:33:00,381 --> 00:33:02,420
England watched, wary and alarmed,

541
00:33:02,520 --> 00:33:05,480
as troops on both sides became entrenched in what promised

542
00:33:05,580 --> 00:33:08,780
to be a long defensive war.

543
00:33:08,880 --> 00:33:11,660
Officially, America remained neutral.

544
00:33:11,760 --> 00:33:14,520
American businesses were prohibited from selling

545
00:33:14,620 --> 00:33:18,000
armaments to the combatants.

546
00:33:18,100 --> 00:33:20,600
But Schwab couldn't be bothered with the fine print

547
00:33:20,700 --> 00:33:24,000
of foreign policy, not when there was money to be made.

548
00:33:26,740 --> 00:33:30,080
Sailing to England under the name Alexander McDonald

549
00:33:30,081 --> 00:33:33,880
to avoid detection, Schwab met with two men who would change

550
00:33:33,980 --> 00:33:38,080
the fate of Bethlehem Steel, Lord Horatio Kitchener

551
00:33:38,180 --> 00:33:40,720
and a young Winston Churchill.

552
00:33:40,820 --> 00:33:42,700
Schwab convinced Kitchener and Churchill

553
00:33:42,800 --> 00:33:47,940
to buy all their steel plate and armaments from his company.

554
00:33:48,040 --> 00:33:50,460
A lucrative deal in and of itself,

555
00:33:50,560 --> 00:33:52,760
but there was more to come.

556
00:33:52,860 --> 00:33:56,040
Bethlehem had recently acquired the Four Rivers shipbuilding

557
00:33:56,041 --> 00:33:59,780
for $750,000.

558
00:33:59,880 --> 00:34:04,540
During peacetime, a submarine sold for $250,000.

559
00:34:04,640 --> 00:34:07,480
Schwab convinced the English to order 20 of them

560
00:34:07,580 --> 00:34:11,880
at an astounding price of $500,000 apiece.

561
00:34:11,980 --> 00:34:14,060
If Schwab could pull it off, the deal

562
00:34:14,160 --> 00:34:16,320
would be the crowning moment of his career.

563
00:34:16,420 --> 00:34:19,800
But he would have to find a way around the official position

564
00:34:19,900 --> 00:34:22,300
of the US government.

565
00:34:22,400 --> 00:34:25,040
Schwab's solution was to build the parts in America,

566
00:34:25,041 --> 00:34:27,540
but assembled them in England.

567
00:34:27,640 --> 00:34:30,040
It was a sleight of hand that added great wealth

568
00:34:30,140 --> 00:34:33,240
to the Bethlehem coffers and made his company

569
00:34:33,340 --> 00:34:36,280
a true competitor to his rival, US Steel.

570
00:34:40,880 --> 00:34:43,600
Many believed that the end of World War I

571
00:34:43,700 --> 00:34:46,200
would bring an end to the lucrative expansion

572
00:34:46,300 --> 00:34:48,000
of the steel industry.

573
00:34:48,100 --> 00:34:50,400
But Schwab and other American steelmakers

574
00:34:50,500 --> 00:34:53,340
found that the country was changing.

575
00:34:53,341 --> 00:34:56,840
New opportunities were opening up as people moved

576
00:34:56,940 --> 00:34:59,280
from the country to the city.

577
00:34:59,380 --> 00:35:03,280
As people came off the farm for World War I

578
00:35:03,380 --> 00:35:06,880
to work in the factories, there needed to be canned food,

579
00:35:06,980 --> 00:35:10,700
food that could be preserved for a while in urban areas.

580
00:35:10,800 --> 00:35:14,600
People were no longer able to produce most of their food

581
00:35:14,700 --> 00:35:16,700
and consume most of their food on the farms.

582
00:35:16,800 --> 00:35:20,840
There had to be a way to take that food, to process it,

583
00:35:20,841 --> 00:35:23,880
and to bring it to an urban market.

584
00:35:23,980 --> 00:35:27,340
The new way was tin-plated steel cans.

585
00:35:27,440 --> 00:35:30,280
A new company, Campbell Soup, put in a contract

586
00:35:30,380 --> 00:35:34,580
to order 500 million cans per year.

587
00:35:34,680 --> 00:35:38,260
Swords of war were being turned not into plowshares,

588
00:35:38,360 --> 00:35:39,760
but into consumer goods.

589
00:35:43,460 --> 00:35:50,600
So this was a great period, a period of expansion

590
00:35:50,601 --> 00:35:52,600
into new markets.

591
00:35:52,601 --> 00:35:57,600
The steel industry was able to supply the materials

592
00:35:57,601 --> 00:36:03,580
that led to the great expansions in construction.

593
00:36:03,581 --> 00:36:06,580
And these looked like good times.

594
00:36:06,581 --> 00:36:10,580
It looked like the good times would never end.

595
00:36:10,581 --> 00:36:13,600
A man named Ford was making a fortune selling cars

596
00:36:13,601 --> 00:36:17,600
to the middle class.

597
00:36:17,601 --> 00:36:21,400
But the Great Depression changed the boom times of the 20s

598
00:36:21,500 --> 00:36:22,200
to bust.

599
00:36:25,600 --> 00:36:29,340
The once vibrant Wall Street was now in trouble,

600
00:36:29,440 --> 00:36:34,820
and money for large building projects virtually disappeared.

601
00:36:34,920 --> 00:36:37,480
Men were laid off by the thousands

602
00:36:37,580 --> 00:36:39,960
as the grip of poverty wrapped its fingers

603
00:36:40,060 --> 00:36:43,200
around the once strong and powerful industry.

604
00:36:43,300 --> 00:36:46,460
The Depression would prove disastrous for many Americans,

605
00:36:46,461 --> 00:36:49,460
most particularly for Charles Schwab.

606
00:36:49,560 --> 00:36:51,460
Charlie throughout his life was a gambler.

607
00:36:51,560 --> 00:36:53,460
He was a gambler in his business deals,

608
00:36:53,560 --> 00:36:55,800
and he was a gambler in personal life.

609
00:36:55,900 --> 00:36:58,400
He was very involved in the stock market,

610
00:36:58,500 --> 00:37:02,300
and it was later shown that he lost lots of money there.

611
00:37:02,400 --> 00:37:05,440
By the late 30s, he was essentially out of money.

612
00:37:05,540 --> 00:37:08,780
And in his final years of life, he died in 1939.

613
00:37:08,880 --> 00:37:13,280
He actually borrowed money from the order of priests that

614
00:37:13,380 --> 00:37:16,460
taught him in his little town of Laredo.

615
00:37:16,461 --> 00:37:25,100
Now, America's next great conflict was about to start a new chapter in the story of steel.

616
00:37:25,101 --> 00:37:32,039
The total world production of steel is over 450 million tons annually.

617
00:37:32,040 --> 00:37:34,220
Over a quarter of this

618
00:37:34,221 --> 00:37:36,369
is produced in the United States.

619
00:37:36,370 --> 00:37:39,820
Modern Marvel's Then and Now will return in a moment.

620
00:37:39,821 --> 00:37:47,020
American Steel now continues on Modern Marvels,

621
00:37:47,120 --> 00:37:47,920
then and now.

622
00:37:51,700 --> 00:37:54,540
As America began to struggle out of the Great Depression,

623
00:37:54,640 --> 00:37:57,180
the steel industry was desperately trying to stay

624
00:37:57,280 --> 00:37:57,980
profitable.

625
00:38:00,640 --> 00:38:03,840
World War II once again boosted demand.

626
00:38:03,940 --> 00:38:06,080
During the war, Franklin Roosevelt

627
00:38:06,180 --> 00:38:08,780
had dispensed with labor management conflicts

628
00:38:08,781 --> 00:38:12,140
by fixing the price of steel and demanding a halt

629
00:38:12,240 --> 00:38:13,620
to all labor strikes.

630
00:38:16,920 --> 00:38:20,120
The plan was successful since so much steel was produced

631
00:38:20,220 --> 00:38:24,960
that profits were handsome and overtime was plentiful.

632
00:38:25,060 --> 00:38:29,000
Besides, labor and management could put aside their differences

633
00:38:29,100 --> 00:38:32,500
to fight a common enemy overseas.

634
00:38:32,600 --> 00:38:35,600
But the end of the war ended the overtime.

635
00:38:35,601 --> 00:38:38,840
Returning soldiers created a plentiful supply of labor.

636
00:38:38,940 --> 00:38:42,340
The war's end also unleashed a flurry of pent-up consumer

637
00:38:42,440 --> 00:38:45,540
demand and created an explosion of building.

638
00:38:45,640 --> 00:38:49,540
And steel was a key ingredient.

639
00:38:49,640 --> 00:38:53,620
By the early 1950s, 85% of all manufactured goods

640
00:38:53,720 --> 00:38:56,020
contained at least some steel.

641
00:38:56,120 --> 00:38:58,620
But despite the demand, steel workers

642
00:38:58,720 --> 00:39:00,920
saw their wages stay the same.

643
00:39:00,921 --> 00:39:02,420
The same.

644
00:39:04,620 --> 00:39:07,500
Roosevelt's policy of price fixing during the war

645
00:39:07,600 --> 00:39:10,760
was disastrous in a peacetime economy.

646
00:39:10,860 --> 00:39:13,060
Profits for other industries rose,

647
00:39:13,160 --> 00:39:16,740
but steel was stuck despite the fact that demand had never

648
00:39:16,840 --> 00:39:19,940
been higher.

649
00:39:20,040 --> 00:39:23,280
Tin coated steel cans were a good example.

650
00:39:23,380 --> 00:39:27,740
Over 50 million of them were used every day, over 17 billion

651
00:39:27,840 --> 00:39:29,140
a year.

652
00:39:30,920 --> 00:39:34,220
Facing a greater demand and desire to boost profits even

653
00:39:34,320 --> 00:39:38,500
higher, steel companies looked at labor as the natural place

654
00:39:38,600 --> 00:39:40,360
to cut costs.

655
00:39:40,460 --> 00:39:44,000
But the steel worker wasn't about to give up without a fight.

656
00:39:44,100 --> 00:39:46,260
And for the first time, the unions

657
00:39:46,360 --> 00:39:49,080
would receive support from the federal government.

658
00:39:53,280 --> 00:39:57,420
The steel workers were ready to take on the owners once again.

659
00:39:57,520 --> 00:40:00,920
And it would be a confrontation with disastrous results

660
00:40:00,921 --> 00:40:03,060
for the world's strongest industry.

661
00:40:13,460 --> 00:40:15,860
Through the late 50s, labor and management

662
00:40:15,960 --> 00:40:18,780
struggled to reach an agreement.

663
00:40:18,880 --> 00:40:23,780
By 1959, the conflict had escalated into a crisis,

664
00:40:23,880 --> 00:40:27,020
and both sides left the bargaining table vowing not

665
00:40:27,120 --> 00:40:29,480
to return.

666
00:40:29,481 --> 00:40:32,580
That year, one of the longest, most devastating strikes

667
00:40:32,680 --> 00:40:34,720
in the history of the steel industry

668
00:40:34,820 --> 00:40:38,820
virtually shut down production for over four months.

669
00:40:38,920 --> 00:40:42,260
Building came to a standstill, and the lives of over half

670
00:40:42,360 --> 00:40:44,920
a million workers were put on hold.

671
00:40:45,020 --> 00:40:47,240
120 days went by.

672
00:40:47,340 --> 00:40:48,940
There was no income.

673
00:40:49,040 --> 00:40:52,900
The union tried to muster up and meet at Morrell Park.

674
00:40:53,000 --> 00:40:55,680
And at times, they would give out a ham or a turkey

675
00:40:55,780 --> 00:40:57,240
and some canned goods.

676
00:40:57,241 --> 00:41:01,180
and that's kind of the way you survive.

677
00:41:01,280 --> 00:41:04,080
As the strike wore on, it became clear to the steel

678
00:41:04,180 --> 00:41:07,180
executives that labor was determined.

679
00:41:07,280 --> 00:41:09,380
This time, the men would hold out.

680
00:41:09,480 --> 00:41:12,780
The mills were losing big money, and labor knew it.

681
00:41:12,880 --> 00:41:16,300
And this time, the government was on labor's side.

682
00:41:16,400 --> 00:41:18,560
Well, I think what finally allowed steel workers

683
00:41:18,660 --> 00:41:21,240
to organize was the kind of legislation that was coming out

684
00:41:21,340 --> 00:41:24,340
of the Roosevelt administration.

685
00:41:24,440 --> 00:41:26,640
After the Depression, the steel corporations

686
00:41:26,641 --> 00:41:32,080
were much more willing to give to labor for the sake

687
00:41:32,180 --> 00:41:33,880
of labor peace.

688
00:41:33,980 --> 00:41:36,720
Finally, management gave in, and the workers'

689
00:41:36,820 --> 00:41:38,380
demands were met.

690
00:41:38,480 --> 00:41:41,720
But the promises made to labor in the late 50s

691
00:41:41,820 --> 00:41:44,860
would come back to haunt the steel industry in the 60s

692
00:41:44,960 --> 00:41:45,660
and 70s.

693
00:41:45,661 --> 00:41:59,661
In the early 1960s, American steel, once the leader in the world,

694
00:42:00,700 --> 00:42:05,580
found itself facing new competition.

695
00:42:05,680 --> 00:42:12,040
The American consumer first was exposed to foreign steel

696
00:42:12,041 --> 00:42:17,380
In 1957, as a result of the US steel strike,

697
00:42:17,480 --> 00:42:20,440
this was the first time that North American consumers

698
00:42:20,540 --> 00:42:23,880
had a chance to sample foreign steels.

699
00:42:23,980 --> 00:42:26,280
And they found, much to their surprise,

700
00:42:26,380 --> 00:42:30,000
that the steel was rather good quality.

701
00:42:30,100 --> 00:42:35,500
This, of course, set the stage for the future of imported

702
00:42:35,600 --> 00:42:40,540
steel into the country, which exists even to today.

703
00:42:40,541 --> 00:42:45,740
The 60s and 70s saw a once proud industry floundering, fat,

704
00:42:45,840 --> 00:42:49,820
unhealthy, and unable to respond to the evolving needs

705
00:42:49,920 --> 00:42:52,780
of a rapidly changing marketplace.

706
00:42:52,880 --> 00:42:56,120
Thousands lost their jobs as mills closed

707
00:42:56,220 --> 00:43:00,360
and small towns once vibrant and robust boarded up their main

708
00:43:00,460 --> 00:43:01,820
streets and their shops.

709
00:43:10,540 --> 00:43:17,540
In the late 70s and early 80s, the steel industry realized

710
00:43:17,640 --> 00:43:20,580
that to stay competitive in the new world marketplace,

711
00:43:20,680 --> 00:43:23,580
attitudes would have to change.

712
00:43:25,520 --> 00:43:28,060
Those of us that did survive had to change your philosophy.

713
00:43:28,160 --> 00:43:30,600
You had to come with the modern times.

714
00:43:30,700 --> 00:43:33,200
You had to realize that if you were going to be a survivor,

715
00:43:33,300 --> 00:43:35,960
you had to do more with less.

716
00:43:35,961 --> 00:43:40,200
to be a survivor, you had to do more with less.

717
00:43:40,300 --> 00:43:44,600
Today, steel companies spend large amounts of money

718
00:43:44,700 --> 00:43:49,540
on equipment and staff to provide high quality steel.

719
00:43:52,540 --> 00:43:54,980
By the late 80s, America was once again

720
00:43:55,080 --> 00:43:57,780
the leading supplier of the world's most vital

721
00:43:57,880 --> 00:44:01,620
manufacturing material.

722
00:44:01,621 --> 00:44:07,420
Steel allows you to build various devices, structures,

723
00:44:07,520 --> 00:44:11,960
machines at low cost.

724
00:44:12,060 --> 00:44:15,260
So steel is still going to play a central role

725
00:44:15,360 --> 00:44:19,480
in the advancing technology of the next century.

726
00:44:19,580 --> 00:44:20,980
That is for sure.

727
00:44:21,080 --> 00:44:23,440
Today, a new artisan is at work,

728
00:44:23,540 --> 00:44:25,940
manufacturing American steel.

729
00:44:26,040 --> 00:44:29,320
Steel production is a high-tech, complex process

730
00:44:29,321 --> 00:44:32,140
where the artist uses a mouse rather than a ladle,

731
00:44:32,400 --> 00:44:35,120
and a keyboard has replaced the thick wool jackets

732
00:44:35,121 --> 00:44:37,440
and the groove-tinted goggles of the steel maker.

733
00:44:38,300 --> 00:44:40,600
Steel has joined the information age,

734
00:44:41,000 --> 00:44:43,740
but as the industrialization of America continues,

735
00:44:44,420 --> 00:44:49,380
steel is still the best material for products built to last.

